  "worked_solution": "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(1,-7).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3+4x}{2-3x} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{17}{(2-3x)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(1) & =17\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(1,-7)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(-7,1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(-7) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(-7))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
